  "Most project reports are read once and forgotten. Here is how to structure reporting so it drives action, not just documents activity.": "",
  "Most project reports suffer from the same problem: they document what happened, but they do not drive what should happen next. Stakeholders skim them, file them, and move on. The report becomes a ritual, not a tool.\n\nHere is how to structure project reporting so it accelerates decisions instead of just documenting activity.\n\n## The Problem with Traditional Status Reports\n\nA typical status report looks like this:\n\n> \"Completed tasks A, B, and C. Working on D and E. No major issues. On track.\"\n\nThis tells stakeholders nothing they can act on. It provides comfort but no clarity. The three common failures:\n\n1. **Activity-focused, not decision-focused**: Reports list what was done, not what needs deciding\n2. **No explicit asks**: Stakeholders do not know what is expected of them\n3. **Vague status indicators**: \"On track\" means different things to different people\n\n## The Decision-Centric Report Structure\n\nEvery weekly report should answer five questions:\n\n### 1. What was completed this week?\nBe specific. \"Finalized vendor shortlist (3 candidates)\" is better than \"Worked on vendor selection.\"\n\n### 2. What is planned for next week?\nAgain, specific. \"Send RFP to 3 shortlisted vendors\" not \"Continue vendor process.\"\n\n### 3. What decisions are needed — and from whom?\nThis is the most important section. Example:\n\n| Decision | Needed from | Deadline | Impact if delayed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Approve vendor RFP budget | CFO | 28 May | RFP send delayed by 1 week |\n| Confirm launch date | CEO | 30 May | Marketing campaign timing affected |\n\n### 4. What risks or blockers need attention?\nUse a simple RAID format:\n\n- **Risks**: Things that might go wrong (with mitigation)\n- **Assumptions**: Things we are counting on (that need validation)\n- **Issues**: Things that have already gone wrong (with action plan)\n- **Dependencies**: Things we need from others (with deadline)\n\n### 5. What changed from the plan?\nIf timeline, scope, or budget shifted, say so explicitly. Surprises at the end of a project are almost always visible in weekly reports — if the reports are structured to surface them.\n\n## The One-Page Rule\n\nIf your weekly report is longer than one page, it will not be read. Force brevity:\n\n- Bullet points, not paragraphs\n- Tables for decisions and RAID items\n- Red/Amber/Green indicators only where they tie to a specific action\n- No background or context that stakeholders already know\n\n## Reporting Cadence That Works\n\n| Audience | Frequency | Focus |\n|---|---|---|\n| Core project team | Daily standup (15 min) | Tactical: who is doing what today, any blockers |\n| Project sponsor / executive | Weekly (1-page report) | Decisions needed, risks, plan vs. actual |\n| Steering committee | Monthly (presentation + report) | Strategic: budget, timeline, scope, major decisions |\n\n## The Real Test\n\nAfter your next report, ask each stakeholder: \"What action did you take based on this report?\" If they cannot answer, the report failed — regardless of how well it was written.\n\n## How We Apply This\n\nEvery Best Visual project engagement includes weekly executive reporting structured around decisions, RAID items, and plan-vs-actual tracking. Our clients know what is happening, what is next, and what they need to decide — every week, without exception.\n\n[Contact us](/contact) if you want structured project coordination for your next initiative.": "",
